  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930102",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-141812",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c3e24f9c-d383-5b77-a22a-8e142a072f0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square",
      "problem": "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-8x=20.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-8x+16$ is a perfect square.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+16=(x-4{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}-16 & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $16$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2}-16+16 & =20+16 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |x-4| & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4-6=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-2{)}^{2}+{10}^{2}=104.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "100",
              "option": "0",
