    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-110042",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Units of a Derivative",
      "problem": "A battery lasts $L(t)$ hours when it is charged for $t$ minutes. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We have that the battery duration $L(t)$ is measured in hours and the charging time is measured in minutes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t): & \\text{hours} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}$ measures a change in $L(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{minute}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}$ is measured in \"hours per minute\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "minutes",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "hours per minute",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "squared hours",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "hours",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "minutes per hour",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
